(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-antiarrhythmic-classes-1a-quiz"]={"title":"Antiarrhythmic Classes & Class IA Drugs Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic class has only ventricular arrhythmias among its listed indications?","choices":["Class IA","Class III","Class IB","Class IV"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Class IB is listed for ventricular tachycardia, ventricular fibrillation prevention and symptomatic ventricular beats, all ventricular. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which risk is shared by every antiarrhythmic drug?","choices":["They prolong the action potential","They may induce arrhythmias","They cause bronchospasm","They decrease contractility"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Minimizing risk is a principle of therapy because all antiarrhythmics may induce arrhythmias, so the mechanism has to be understood before one is chosen. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which indirect action does quinidine have at higher doses?","choices":["Beta blockade","Calcium channel blockade","Potassium channel activation","Alpha blockade"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Quinidine's indirect actions are anticholinergic effects and, at higher doses, alpha blockade, which helps explain its hypotension. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Class IA, IB and IC antiarrhythmics all act on which target?","choices":["Potassium channels","Calcium channels","Beta receptors","Sodium channels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Class 1 drugs are the sodium channel blockers; the subclasses A, B and C differ in how they affect the channel and the action potential. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which eye condition contraindicates disopyramide?","choices":["Corneal deposits","Glaucoma","Nystagmus","Blurred vision from cinchonism"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Disopyramide is contraindicated in heart block, hypersensitivity and glaucoma; its strong anticholinergic action explains the glaucoma contraindication. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Compared with quinidine, how does procainamide differ?","choices":["More anticholinergic, negative inotrope","Greater affinity for ischemic tissue","Added beta-blocking activity","Less anticholinergic, no alpha block"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Procainamide has less anticholinergic activity than quinidine and no alpha-blocking activity. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which is a listed indication for quinidine?","choices":["Slowing the ventricular rate in atrial fibrillation","Terminating torsades de pointes","Ending paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia","Maintaining rhythm in atrial fibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Quinidine is listed to maintain rhythm with atrial flutter or fibrillation and to control ventricular tachycardia; its use for conversion is very limited. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic class does disopyramide belong to?","choices":["Class IA","Class IB","Class IC","Class III"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Disopyramide is a class IA sodium channel blocker with effects similar to quinidine, more anticholinergic action and a negative inotropic effect. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Quinidine's listed drug interaction comes from inhibiting which enzyme?","choices":["Sodium-potassium ATPase","Carbonic anhydrase","Angiotensin-converting enzyme","Cytochrome P450 2D6"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Quinidine is a cytochrome P450 2D6 (CYP2D6) inhibitor, and codeine and morphine are the named examples affected. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic class is listed for refractory ventricular tachycardia as well as atrial fibrillation and flutter?","choices":["Class IB","Class III","Class IV","Class II"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Class III is listed for refractory ventricular tachycardia, atrial fibrillation, flutter and supraventricular tachycardia, so it covers both sides. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic class prolongs repolarization by acting on potassium?","choices":["Class I","Class II","Class III","Class IV"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Class 3 drugs prolong repolarization by acting on potassium (they reduce potassium efflux), which lengthens the action potential. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which adverse effect of quinidine occurs in 30 to 50 percent of patients?","choices":["Torsades de pointes","Gastrointestinal upset","Thrombocytopenia","Cinchonism"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Gastrointestinal upset (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea) is listed in 30 to 50 percent, quinidine's most frequent toxicity. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which patients taking procainamide are more likely to develop a lupus-like syndrome?","choices":["Slow acetylators","Fast acetylators","Patients given it intravenously","Patients with glaucoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lupus-like syndrome (25 to 50 percent) is more often seen in slow acetylators. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which arrhythmia occurs in 2 to 8 percent of patients taking quinidine?","choices":["Paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia","Complete heart block","Torsades de pointes","Sinus bradycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Torsades de pointes is listed in 2 to 8 percent of patients on quinidine, alongside quinidine sudden death. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Why is procainamide made as a slow-release preparation?","choices":["Its action lasts only 3 to 4 hours","It undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism","Its half-life is about 40 days","It is eliminated unchanged by the kidneys"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Procainamide has a short duration of action (3 to 4 hours), so a slow-release preparation is used. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Why can disopyramide cause congestive heart failure?","choices":["It is strongly anticholinergic","It blocks alpha receptors","It has a negative inotropic effect","It blocks potassium channels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Disopyramide has negative inotropic effects, and its toxicity list gives congestive heart failure from decreased contractility. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which electrical change in working heart cells defines class IA drugs?","choices":["Slow phase 0 and prolong the action potential","Shorten phase 3 mainly in ischemic tissue","Prolong phase 3 with phase 0 unchanged","Decrease phase 4 in nodal pacemaker cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Class IA drugs such as quinidine slow the rate of rise of phase 0 and slow conduction, and the class IA picture shows a longer action potential. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 8"}]};
